A||||8||Gokart||Couldn't get oil pan off!!||||8||
Z||000000||Gokart||11-29-1999||08:05 PM||||Yesterday I changed the starter in my GT and noticed a small leak coming from my oil pan, so I figured while I had the starter off I'd change the gasket. BIG MISTAKE! I had all the bolts off and had to take the spring off the muffler to lower it and still couldn't get it off. It's hitting an engine mount on the right (facing the rear of the car) and it won't come all the way out! So I had to put it back on, and of course now it's leaking worse than before. Could someone PLEASE tell me where I went wrong. Maybe I have the wrong oil pan? I know I can't loosen the engine mount to get it out, right?!?||||reg||8
Z||000001||chris6878||11-29-1999||08:26 PM||||never took an oilpan off of a fier when the engine wasnt out...   but  i have several times on my dad's buick somerset.. 2.5 4cyl..<P>had to take the front motor mt.. off and jack up the engine for the pan to clear the mount...  u may have to do the same with your fiero..  u have limited space down there..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000002||chris6878||11-29-1999||08:27 PM||||never took an oilpan off of a fiero when the engine wasnt out...   but  i have several times on my dad's buick somerset.. 2.5 4cyl..<P>had to take the front motor mt.. off and jack up the engine for the pan to clear the mount...  u may have to do the same with your fiero..  u have limited space down there..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000003||chris6878||11-29-1999||08:27 PM||||oppps.. sorry||||reg||9
Z||000004||BigBoyToys||11-29-1999||08:49 PM||||I ran into this same problem this past summer <BR>it is not as straight forward as the Haynes<BR>manual states. I actually removed about 10mm<BR>off the engine mount. I took off a little bit at time using a cut-off tool just above the bend. Also notice that the engine crankshaft must rotate so that the connecting rod part of the crank is tucked up inside the block, try to picture the ofset of the crankshaft at its low point in the oil pan and how this can  pinch the oil pan in this area. Once I had the crank up, started off, exhaust pipe out of the way and as I shaved the mount it lierally fell off. From what I saw I do not believe that I have degraded the engine mount at all, and by taking material off a little at a time the mount never gets to hot.<BR>Remember to touch up the bare metal now exposed to prevent rust. I also purchased a gasket set for the oil pan rather than just RTV as the manuals suggest. Good Luck and go slow.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||sluppy123||11-29-1999||08:50 PM||||Take a look at this thread.<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001450.html" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001450.html</A> <P>Brian<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gokart||11-29-1999||09:21 PM||||Damn that Haynes manual, they always make jobs look easier than meets the eye. I don't know if I'm up for the abuse next weekend that I had to go through this last one. May take it to the dealer. Thanks guys!||||reg||8
Z||000007||dogtired||11-30-1999||11:15 AM||||1st read the above thread.<BR>Now an option that worked for me. I took 2 pieces of 4X4 lumber about 1 foot long each, placed them on top of my shock towers, then put a 2X6 across the engine compatrment each end resting on the 4X4s. I then jacked up the motor about 3inches and chained it to that 2X6, and removed the jack. That way after removing starter, exhaust and the front motor mount (actually the one on the right side of the motor) the pan comes off fairly easy? You will NOT need to FORCE anything! Especially getting the pan past the oil pump pick-up. If you screwup the oil-pump pick-up you will not have sufficient oil pressure, which will give you the opportunity to get real familiar with all the other internal workings of the motor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Good luck. If this is as clear as mud let me know & I will try to explain better?||||reg||6
Z||000008||MCordray||11-30-1999||07:11 PM||||I took the oil pan off of my '86 GT without removing the exhaust. I removed the starter, removed the bolts to the right motor mount, jacked-up the engine about 6-8 inches, and after removing the bolts, the pan came right out. It was tight, but I was able to get it off without any parts damage. Hardest part was getting the motor mount to line back up.||||reg||1
